IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `jcca-party`

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 -- worked cases, from the seat of the party to a civil proceeding.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (C2023C00038), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the
-- facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every opinion of a court is given as a fact.
--
-- A breached DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the regulative rule is asserted through
-- CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and the breach is also #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 4. HYPOTHETICAL: Priya sues her former employer in the Supreme Court of New South Wales for negligence (State law, the
-- court's own jurisdiction) and adds a claim under a Commonwealth Act that only the Federal Court could otherwise hear.
-- The negligence claim: the court has it anyway.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` TRUE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, without this Act: the court has jurisdiction over the matter apart from section 4`
-- The Commonwealth claim: s 4(1)(c) invests the NSW Supreme Court with federal jurisdiction.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the State Supreme Court is invested with federal jurisdiction over the matter, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(c))`
-- The same claim filed in the ACT Supreme Court: conferred, s 4(1)(d) (the ACT is a "State" under s 3(1), but s 4(1)(d) names it).
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on the Territory Supreme Court, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(d))`
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of an external Territory` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on the Territory Supreme Court, because the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) has jurisdiction over it (s 4(1)(d))`
-- Priya's general protections claim under the Fair Work Act 2009: s 4 does not apply (s 4(4)(ab)).
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` FALSE FALSE `the Fair Work Act 2009` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 does not apply to a matter arising under the law I named (s 4(4))`
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` FALSE FALSE `the Native Title Act 1993` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 does not apply to a matter arising under the law I named (s 4(4))`
-- Criminal: not a proceeding (s 3(1)).
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` FALSE TRUE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 confers jurisdiction over civil matters only, and a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1), s 4(1), (2))`
-- s 4(1) does not reach a State Family Court (s 3(2) excludes s 4(1)): no.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 gives this court no jurisdiction over the matter on these facts`
-- s 4(2): a Norfolk Island matter, filed in the Federal Court; and in the Family Court of WA (s 3(2) applies to s 4(2)).
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on this court, because the Supreme Court of an external Territory has jurisdiction over it (s 4(2))`
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: jurisdiction over the matter is conferred on this court, because the Supreme Court of an external Territory has jurisdiction over it (s 4(2))`
-- s 4(3): transferred to the Federal Court.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my proceeding was transferred to this court, which has jurisdiction over every matter in it that it would not otherwise have (s 4(3))`
-- Transferred to a State Supreme Court: s 4(3) names only the two federal courts and a State Family Court.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 gives this court no jurisdiction over the matter on these facts`
-- A Federal Court matter filed in the Federal Court lacking jurisdiction otherwise: nothing in s 4 (s 4(1) gives to Supreme Courts).
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 4 gives this court no jurisdiction over the matter on these facts`
-- A district court: s 4 does not speak to it.
#ASSERT `Can this court hear my matter question` `another court or tribunal` FALSE FALSE `none of the laws section 4(4) lists` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 5. HYPOTHETICAL: Tom is injured in a Queensland road crash and sues in the Supreme Court of Victoria, where he lives;
-- the other driver has already sued in the Supreme Court of Queensland. The Victorian court thinks Queensland more appropriate.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
-- No related proceeding, but on the s 5(2)(b)(ii) weighing Queensland is more appropriate.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
-- None of the grounds appears to the court: no transfer required.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`
-- Supreme Court to the Federal Court, interests of justice (s 5(1)(b)(iii)).
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(1))`
-- The Family Court of WA counts as WA's Supreme Court for s 5(1) (s 3(2)).
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(1))`
-- Between WA's Supreme Court and the Family Court of WA (s 5(3)), both ways.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the State Family Court of the same State` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the State's Supreme Court and State Family Court (s 5(3))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` `the Supreme Court of the same State` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the State's Supreme Court and State Family Court (s 5(3))`
-- Federal Court to a Supreme Court (s 5(4)).
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
-- Between the Federal Court and the FCFCOA (s 5(5)): ground (ii) of the other subsections does not exist there.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Federal Court and the FCFCOA (Division 1) (s 5(5))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`
-- A special federal matter in a Supreme Court: s 6 governs.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `section 6 governs instead: my proceeding in a Supreme Court involves a special federal matter`
-- s 6A cases.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `the Supreme Court of a (different) State or Territory` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so s 5(4) does not apply; the federal court may transfer my federal matter proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(2))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so neither s 5(1) nor s 6 requires the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
-- Federal Court to the Family Court of WA: s 5(4), read with s 3(2) (a State Family Court is a Supreme Court of a State there).
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `the State Family Court of the same State` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the court must transfer it to that Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
-- Pairs s 5 does not join: a district court to anywhere; the Federal Court to the "same State" Supreme Court (no State is the same).
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `the Supreme Court of the same State` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding be transferred question` `another court or tribunal` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

-- s 5(6): related proceeding follows.
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow question` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer my related proceeding to the same court (s 5(6))`
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 5(6) requires it only where the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law and it appears to the court that my related proceeding should, in the interests of justice, be decided by the other court`
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 5(6) requires it only where the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law and it appears to the court that my related proceeding should, in the interests of justice, be decided by the other court`

-- s 5(7): Tom, the plaintiff, may apply; so may an intervener; a stranger has no application under the Act.
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: as a party (an intervener counts) I may apply; the court may also act of its own motion, and under section 5 the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory may apply (ss 3(1), 5(7), 8(1), 10)`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer question` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `the person is not a party to the proceeding (a party includes an intervener, s 3(1)); this Act gives a non-party no application`

-- s 5(8): Tom's Victorian solicitor after transfer to Queensland.
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer keep acting question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: my lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, for my transferred proceeding and any related proceeding decided with it, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer keep acting question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 5(8) carries over only a right to practise that my lawyer had in the court the proceeding came from, and only on a transfer under a cross-vesting law`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer keep acting question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 5(8) carries over only a right to practise that my lawyer had in the court the proceeding came from, and only on a transfer under a cross-vesting law`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 6. HYPOTHETICAL: a small business sues a supplier in the Supreme Court of South Australia for misuse of market power
-- (Competition and Consumer Act 2010 Part IV, s 46), which the Supreme Court could not hear apart from this Act.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices), other than s 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
-- The Supreme Court made a s 6(3) order: it keeps it.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices), other than s 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court has ordered, for special reasons, that it determine the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it can do so only after notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (s 6(4))`
-- If the Supreme Court had jurisdiction apart from this Act, it is not a special federal matter (closing words of the definition).
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
-- (b): a question of law from a Commonwealth tribunal; and where a Commonwealth Act sends that appeal to a Supreme Court, not special.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
-- (ab) Family Law Act s 60G: in the Supreme Court of Tasmania it goes to whichever family court is appropriate; in the Family Court of WA it is not special.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the receiving court's jurisdiction to whichever of the FCFCOA (Division 1), the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the matter is not a special federal matter (s 3(1))`
-- (aa) Competition Code: special, but s 6(2) names no receiving court (Finding 2).
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory` `(aa) the Competition Code of the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, but the text names no receiving court: the matter is a special federal matter under paragraph (aa), which s 6(2) does not mention`
-- s 6(10): appeal where the court below made a s 6(3) order.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 6 does not apply to this appeal, because the court below made an order under s 6(3) (s 6(10))`
-- s 6A(3): an ADJR matter with the State proceeding in the same State; but s 6A does not touch a Part IV matter.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 6A applies, so section 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(3))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices), other than s 45D, 45DA, 45DB, 45E or 45EA` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of my proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
-- Not in a Supreme Court.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` `the Federal Court of Australia` `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 6 applies only to a proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of a State or Territory`
-- The definition directly.
#ASSERT `is a special federal matter` `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- s 6(4), (5): notice.
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorneys-General question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court has directed me to give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory (s 6(4)(a), (5)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorneys-General question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not unless the court directs me: the Act names no one who must give the notice, but the court cannot keep the matter until it is satisfied the notice was given and a reasonable time has passed (s 6(4))`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorneys-General question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the notice is needed only where a special federal matter is pending in a Supreme Court that is asked to keep it (s 6(3), (4))`

-- The duty as a rule: directed to give notice within 14 days. Notice on day 14 (the last day), then the court orders on day 40: fulfilled.
`notice on the last day, then the order` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 6(5)(b) -- I must give the notice the court directed` 14) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, the party the court directed` `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory` 14), (EVENT `the Supreme Court` `order that the proceeding be determined by the Supreme Court` 40))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `notice on the last day, then the order` EQUALS TRUE
-- Notice on day 15, one day late: breached, so not fulfilled.
`notice one day late` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 6(5)(b) -- I must give the notice the court directed` 14) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, the party the court directed` `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory` 15))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `notice one day late` EQUALS FALSE
#TRACE `s 6(5)(b) -- I must give the notice the court directed` 14 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `I, the party the court directed` DOES `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State or Territory` AT 15
-- expect: BREACH by the party the court directed, "s 6(5)(b): ... did not give it in the time the court allowed"

-- s 6(5)(a), (7): adjournment costs.
#ASSERT `Can my adjournment costs be met question` TRUE EQUALS `possibly: the court may order costs of the adjournment as it thinks fit (s 6(5)(a)), and the Commonwealth Attorney-General may authorise the Commonwealth to pay me an amount for my costs arising out of it (s 6(7)); both are discretions`
#ASSERT `Can my adjournment costs be met question` FALSE EQUALS `no: sections 6(5)(a) and 6(7) deal only with the costs of an adjournment under section 6 (or a corresponding State provision)`

-- s 6(8): urgent interim injunction.
#ASSERT `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` TRUE EQUALS `yes: section 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting me urgent interlocutory relief, as the court thinks the interests of justice require (s 6(8))`
#ASSERT `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` FALSE EQUALS `section 6 does not stand in the way, but the Supreme Court has not found that the interests of justice call for urgent interlocutory relief (s 6(8))`

-- s 6(9), s 7(8): decisions not invalidated.
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Supreme Court, through inadvertence, decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` EQUALS `answered` `no: nothing in section 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(9))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `the Full Court of a Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court or the High Court (through inadvertence, or because the interests of justice required it to proceed)` EQUALS `answered` `no: nothing in section 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(8))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision invalid question` `something else` EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 6A. HYPOTHETICAL: a Commonwealth body exercising powers under a State Act makes a decision; Lee seeks ADJR review while
-- a related proceeding under the State Act is in a State court.
#ASSERT `What section 6A does for me question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)` EQUALS `section 6A applies: the federal court may transfer my proceeding to the Supreme Court of the State where the related State proceeding is pending, if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2))`
#ASSERT `What section 6A does for me question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `the Supreme Court of the State where the State matter proceeding is pending` EQUALS `section 6A applies: the Supreme Court is not required by s 5(1) or s 6 to transfer my proceeding to a federal court, but may do so if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(3))`
#ASSERT `What section 6A does for me question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `somewhere else` EQUALS `section 6A applies, but it changes nothing for a proceeding in that court; sections 5 and 6 apply as usual`
-- The State proceeding itself has an ADJR matter in it: s 6A(1)(c)(ii) fails.
#ASSERT `What section 6A does for me question` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)` EQUALS `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`
#ASSERT `What section 6A does for me question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE `the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)` EQUALS `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 7. HYPOTHETICAL: Tom loses his road injury claim before a single judge of the Supreme Court of Queensland: only State law.
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a single judge of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `only to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of that State or Territory (s 7(3))`
-- A Supreme Court judge decided a matter under the Bankruptcy Act 1966 (in the Schedule) together with a contract claim.
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a single judge of the Supreme Court of a State or Territory` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires, or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(5))`
-- A judge of the Family Court of WA: Family Law Act matter and a property matter under WA law (s 7(6)).
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a judge of a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `either as s 7(5) says (the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), or the High Court with special leave), or, because the appeal also involves another matter, it may be dealt with as if no Schedule Act matter were in it, that is by the Full Court of the State Family Court (s 7(6), (3))`
-- Family Court of WA, Family Law Act matter only: s 7(5).
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a judge of a State Family Court (the Family Court of Western Australia)` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires, or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(5))`
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a single judge of the Federal Court or the FCFCOA (Division 1)` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `not to the Full Court of a State or Territory Supreme Court (s 7(1)), and not to the other of the two federal courts (s 7(2)); where it does lie is for the Act that created the court`
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a court of summary jurisdiction of a State (a magistrates or local court)` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `not to the Supreme Court of the State: an appeal lies to the State Family Court, so it goes there (s 7(4))`
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `a court of summary jurisdiction of a State (a magistrates or local court)` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`
#ASSERT `Where must I appeal question` `some other court or tribunal` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`
-- The Schedule names thirteen Acts.
#ASSERT count `the Schedule` EQUALS 13

-- s 7(7).
#ASSERT `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the Full Court must transfer my appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the FCFCOA (Division 1), as the case requires (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Full Court may go on to decide my appeal, because the interests of justice require it (s 7(7)); its decision is not invalid for that (s 7(8))`
#ASSERT `What happens to my appeal in the wrong Full Court question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `section 7(7) does not arise: my appeal is not one that s 7(5) sends to a federal Full Court`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 8. HYPOTHETICAL: a claim in the Court of Petty Sessions of Norfolk Island related to a Federal Court case.
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, it may be: the Territory's Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or its own motion; the Act then treats it as pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, it may be: the Territory's Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or its own motion; the Act then treats it as pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 8 lets the Supreme Court remove a proceeding only where one of its grounds appears to it`
-- An ACT Magistrates Court case: the ACT is a State here, so s 8 does not reach it (Finding 4).
#ASSERT `Can my case be removed to the Territory Supreme Court question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 8 is only for a proceeding in a lower court or tribunal of an external Territory (the ACT and the NT are States under this Act)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 10. HYPOTHETICAL: Sam sues a retailer in the Federal Court over an unsafe heater (ACL Part 3-3), a modest claim the
-- Local Court could hear in full.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, it may: the court may transfer my proceeding to that lower court, on my application or its own motion (s 10)`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, it may: the court may transfer my proceeding to that lower court, on my application or its own motion (s 10)`
-- With a Part IV (special federal) matter as well: no.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`
-- An appeal: no.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`
-- The lower court cannot hear every matter: no.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `the Federal Court of Australia` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`
-- Already in a lower court: s 10 does not apply.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower court question` `another court or tribunal` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 10 allows the transfer only if every one of its conditions is met`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 11. Tom's claim, transferred from Victoria to Queensland, under the Queensland Motor Accident Insurance Act (a written law
-- of Queensland): the Queensland court sits in Queensland, so (a) would already point there; but suppose instead the Victorian
-- court kept it and is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction: (b) applies Queensland law.
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the federal court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `the court is not exercising jurisdiction conferred by this Act or by a State cross-vesting law, so s 11 does not say which law it applies`

-- ss 11(3), 12.
#ASSERT `What carries over after transfer question` FALSE EQUALS `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer (s 12)`
#ASSERT `What carries over after transfer question` TRUE EQUALS `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`

-- s 13.
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to` EQUALS `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` EQUALS `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `some other decision` EQUALS `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`

-- s 14.
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory question` `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my federal court judgment is enforceable in an external Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory question` `the Supreme Court of an external Territory` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my Territory Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in that Territory as if given entirely in the court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory question` `the Federal Court of Australia` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the Territory question` `the Supreme Court of one of the six States` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
